course: dictionary
lesson: 14287
title: Noticeably
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Noticeably means in a way that is easy to see, hear or notice. It tells you that a change or difference is big enough for people to spot. «The plant grew noticeably taller in one week.» You can see the difference, so the growth is clear.
explain: Look at the word parts. It comes from notice, plus -able and -ly. To notice something means to see it or become aware of it. So noticeably tells you how easy something is to notice. «She was noticeably quieter after lunch.» Her quietness was easy for everyone to spot.
explain: Use the context to check. Ask: is there a clear change or difference? Then swap in an easy meaning, such as easy to see, and check that the sentence still makes sense. «The water was noticeably colder at the bottom of the pool.» Could you feel the difference easily? Yes, so noticeably fits.
fact: Noticeably means in a way that is easy to see, hear or notice.
fact: «Noticeably» is an adverb, so it tells you how something happens or how big a change is.
fact: Notice is the root word: to notice means to see or become aware of something.
fact: «The plant grew noticeably taller» shows a clear change that you can see.
fact: In comparisons, noticeably says the difference is big enough to see: «The new bridge is noticeably longer than the old one.»
exercise: «The plant grew noticeably taller in one week.» What does noticeably mean here? | Very slowly and quietly | Only a little, hardly at all | Upside down | *In a way that is easy to see
exercise: «The room felt noticeably warmer after the heater came on.» Which word is the root word of noticeably? | heater | *notice | room | warm
exercise: Which word means the same as noticeably? | Slowly | Secretly | *Obviously | Gently
test: «The shop was noticeably busier on Saturday.» What does noticeably tell you? | The shop was closed | *Anyone could easily see the shop was busier | The shop sold only one thing | Nobody went into the shop
test: Which sentence shows a difference big enough to see? | I think cakes are nice. | The two cakes looked the same size. | *Her new cake was noticeably bigger than the old one. | The cake was hidden under a cloth.
exam: Ben says, «The lamp was noticeably brighter.» What does noticeably mean? | Hardly, almost not at all | *In a way that is easy to see | Very, very quietly | Hidden behind something
